+## Due times and going back to 1.4.0
+
+Release 1.5.0 gives every ticket a due time ([decision 9](decisions/0009-due-times.md)). It is stored in the data file with
+the ticket as `due`, and the format stays version 1.
+
+- **Rolling out.** Deploy 1.5.0 with `DUE_TIMES` unset. The first start gives every ticket in the data file its due time
+  (its creation time plus the hours of its priority) and saves the file; the API still answers as 1.4.0 did. Look at the
+  file (`due` on every ticket), then set `DUE_TIMES=on` and restart to show the due times and take `?overdue=true`.
+- **The hours.** `DUE_HOURS_URGENT`, `DUE_HOURS_HIGH`, `DUE_HOURS_NORMAL` and `DUE_HOURS_LOW` (default 4, 24, 72, 168) apply
+  to the tickets created after the restart. A ticket keeps the due time it has.
+- **Going back.** Stop the service, install 1.4.0 and start it on the same data file: 1.4.0 ignores `due` and shows every
+  ticket. Its next save drops the due times from the file. When 1.5.0 starts on that file again it gives the tickets due
+  times again, from their creation time and their priority at that moment, so a ticket whose priority changed in the
+  meantime gets a different due time. Keep a copy of the file from before the rollback if that matters.
+
 ## Notifications
 
 A change that should tell someone something (a ticket received, a ticket resolved) enqueues a notification in the outbox
